### Example 3: Enable Encryption on Existing Volume

Note: EBS volumes cannot be encrypted after creation. To enable encryption, you must:
1. Create snapshot of existing volume
2. Create new encrypted volume from snapshot

```bash
# Step 1: Create snapshot of unencrypted volume
aws ec2 create-snapshot \
  --volume-id vol-0123456789abcdef0 \
  --description "Snapshot for encryption"

# Step 2: Create encrypted volume from snapshot
aws ec2 create-volume \
  --snapshot-id snap-0abcdef1234567890 \
  --availability-zone us-east-1a \
  --volume-type gp3 \
  --encrypted \
  --kms-key-id alias/aws/ebs \
  --tag-specifications 'ResourceType=volume,Tags=[{Key=Name,Value=WebServer-DataDisk-Encrypted}]'

# Step 3: Detach old volume and attach new encrypted volume
# (Requires stopping instance or using multi-attach capable volumes)
```

