## Troubleshooting

### Problem 1: Volume Not Attaching

**Error:** `attachment set is empty` or device not appearing in instance

**Solution:**
```bash
# Verify volume and instance are in same AZ
aws ec2 describe-volumes --volume-ids vol-xxxxx --query "Volumes[0].AvailabilityZone"
aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids i-yyyyy --query "Reservations[0].Instances[0].Placement.AvailabilityZone"

# Wait for attachment to complete
aws ec2 describe-volumes --volume-ids vol-xxxxx --query "Volumes[0].Attachments"

# Reboot instance to make device appear
sudo reboot
```

### Problem 2: Poor Volume Performance

**Symptoms:** High latency, low IOPS, slow disk access

**Solution:**
```bash
# Check current IOPS provisioning
aws ec2 describe-volumes --volume-ids vol-xxxxx \
  --query "Volumes[0].[VolumeType,Iops,Throughput]"

# Monitor actual I/O
sudo iostat -x 1 10
# Look for %util > 80%, avgqu-sz increasing

# For gp3, can modify IOPS without downtime
aws ec2 modify-volume-attribute \
  --volume-id vol-xxxxx \
  --iops 10000

# For other types, may need to create new volume from snapshot
```

### Problem 3: Snapshot Takes Too Long

**Symptoms:** Snapshot stuck in "pending" state for extended time

**Solution:**
```bash
# Check snapshot status
aws ec2 describe-snapshots --snapshot-ids snap-xxxxx

# Large volumes take proportionally longer
# For multi-GB volumes, snapshots can take hours

# Create incremental snapshots more frequently
# First snapshot copies all data, subsequent only changed blocks
```

### Problem 4: Cannot Mount Volume After Reboot

**Symptoms:** Volume not mounted after instance restart

**Solution:**
```bash
# Check /etc/fstab entries
cat /etc/fstab

# Mount manually to verify
sudo mount /dev/xvdf /mnt/data

# Check dmesg for errors
sudo dmesg | tail -20

# Verify device name hasn't changed
lsblk
```

